window.BLOG_DATA = {"generated":"2026-11-06T15:57:44.606Z","defaultNote":"about","about":"about","tree":[{"name":"AI","path":"AI","children":[{"note":"AI/model_ai_vs_agentic_harness"}]},{"name":"Java","path":"Java","children":[{"name":"Collections","path":"Java/Collections","children":[{"note":"Java/Collections/Collections_Framework_Cheatsheet_EN"}]},{"name":"Concurrency","path":"Java/Concurrency","children":[{"note":"Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_EN"}]},{"name":"JVM_Internals","path":"Java/JVM_Internals","children":[{"note":"Java/JVM_Internals/JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_EN"}]},{"name":"Language_Fundamentals","path":"Java/Language_Fundamentals","children":[{"note":"Java/Language_Fundamentals/Exception_Handling_And_IO_EN"}]}]},{"name":"Microservices","path":"Microservices","children":[{"note":"Microservices/Spring Boot - Authentication with JWT_ENG"}]},{"name":"System design","path":"System design","children":[{"note":"System design/Common problems - ENG"}]}],"order":["AI/model_ai_vs_agentic_harness","Java/Collections/Collections_Framework_Cheatsheet_EN","Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_EN","Java/JVM_Internals/JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_EN","Java/Language_Fundamentals/Exception_Handling_And_IO_EN","Microservices/Spring Boot - Authentication with JWT_ENG","System design/Common problems - ENG"],"notes":{"AI/model_ai_vs_agentic_harness":{"title":"model_ai_vs_agentic_harness","category":"AI","date":"","tags":[],"minutes":3,"lead":"","html":"<h1>Model AI vs Agentic Harness</h1>\n<p>Najprościej rzecz ujmując: <strong>Model AI to sam „mózg” (silnik wnioskowania i predykcji), podczas gdy wyodrębnione środowisko Agentic Harness to „ciało” (powłoka wykonawcza), które pozwala temu mózgowi fizycznie działać w zewnętrznym świecie.</strong></p>\n<p>Model sztucznej inteligencji (taki jak Claude 3.5 Sonnet) sam z siebie nie potrafi przeglądać plików, uruchamiać kodu w terminalu ani pamiętać, nad czym pracował godzinę temu. Aby stać się w pełni ukształtowanym, autonomicznym asystentem (np. w rozwiązaniu takim jak Anthropic Claude Code), wymaga struktury <em>Harness</em>, która utrzymuje go w ryzach, dostarcza mu uporządkowanych danych i faktycznie wykonuje jego polecenia w systemie operacyjnym.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. Model AI (Czysty mózg)</h2>\n<p>Sam model językowy wykonuje w rzeczywistości tylko jedną operację: na podstawie dostarczonego tekstu (historii konwersacji) i zdefiniowanej listy dostępnych mu narzędzi, <strong>przewiduje, jakie słowa napisać dalej lub o użycie jakiego narzędzia poprosić</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Brak pamięci:</strong> API modelu jest z natury bezstanowe. Każde nowe zapytanie do Claude to dla niego „czysta karta”.</li><li><strong>Brak możliwości fizycznego działania:</strong> Jeśli model zdecyduje, że zadanie wymaga sprawdzenia pliku <code>main.py</code>, generuje jedynie specjalnie sformatowany obiekt JSON z prośbą o jego otwarcie. Sam nie ma najmniejszej możliwości, by z własnej woli sięgnąć do dysku twardego.</li></ul>\n<h2>2. Agentic Harness (Środowisko wykonawcze)</h2>\n<p>Harness (np. Claude Code, OpenHands czy LangChain Deep Agents) to potężna warstwa oprogramowania otaczająca sam model AI. To ona sprawia, że model staje się agentem potrafiącym realizować asynchroniczne, złożone cele wielokrokowe. Do jej kluczowych modułów należą:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Zarządzanie kontekstem (Pamięć):</strong> Ponieważ okno kontekstowe Claude'a ma fizyczny limit, Harness dynamicznie kompresuje historię, usuwa nieistotne logi, a obszerną dokumentację projektu dostarcza mu porcjami (np. poprzez wektorowe techniki RAG).</li><li><strong>Rejestr i wykonywanie narzędzi:</strong> Gdy Claude prosi w formacie JSON o użycie narzędzia (np. wpisanie komendy w bashu), Harness przechwytuje to żądanie, sprawdza jego poprawność, uruchamia je w wyizolowanym, bezpiecznym środowisku (sandbox), wyciąga wynik z konsoli i przesyła go z powrotem do modelu.</li><li><strong>Bariery bezpieczeństwa (Guardrails):</strong> To właśnie powłoka Harness wstrzymuje wykonanie ryzykownego polecenia (np. instalacji zewnętrznych paczek lub usunięcia głównego repozytorium) i pyta o ludzką zgodę, chroniąc system przed destrukcyjną pomyłką agenta.</li><li><strong>Delegacja zadań (Subagents):</strong> Przy skomplikowanych problemach, główne środowisko może powołać do życia „podagentów” dysponujących czystym kontekstem po to, by rozwiązali oni pomniejszy problem (np. przetestowali osobną klasę), zapobiegając niekontrolowanemu rozrostowi wątku bazowego.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h2>Pętla Agentowa (The Agent Loop)</h2>\n<p>Esencją środowiska Harness jest to, że nie wymusza na użytkowniku podtrzymywania dyskusji. Cały system zamienia się w autonomiczną, zamkniętą pętlę decyzyjną, która kręci się do momentu zakończenia zadania:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Planowanie i Dedukcja (Reason):</strong> Harness podaje modelowi Claude cel, dołączając zawartość katalogu roboczego. Claude analizuje stan aplikacji i obmyśla strategię.</li><li><strong>Działanie (Act):</strong> Claude prosi o narzędzie (np. zapis zmian w kodzie). Harness faktycznie modyfikuje plik na dysku.</li><li><strong>Obserwacja wyników (Observe):</strong> Po użyciu narzędzia Harness odczytuje błędy i wynik kompilacji, przekazuje je z powrotem do głównego okna kontekstowego i wysyła modelowi sygnał do kolejnego kroku. Jeśli kod nie działa, Claude widzi w kolejnym takcie dokładny stack trace i próbuje naprawić błąd.</li></ol>\n<blockquote><p>[!important] Kluczowy wniosek Projektując lub używając agentów AI, nie płacisz po prostu za to, że model jest „niezwykle sprytny”. Zapleczem nowoczesnego programowania agentowego jest przede wszystkim bezawaryjność środowiska Harness — bez wybitnej pętli sprzężenia zwrotnego i rozsądnego zarządzania pamięcią model ugrzęźnie w halucynacjach lub zatrzyma się na najprostszym wyjątku składniowym.</p></blockquote>"},"Java/Collections/Collections_Framework_Cheatsheet_EN":{"title":"Collections Framework — cheatsheet","category":"Java/Collections","date":"2026-11-04","tags":[],"minutes":9,"lead":"","html":"<blockquote><p><strong>TL;DR</strong></p><ul><li><code>Iterable</code> → <code>Collection</code> → <code>List</code> / <code>Set</code> / <code>Queue</code>. <strong><code>Map</code> deliberately does NOT extend <code>Collection</code>.</strong></li><li><code>HashMap</code>: hash → bucket → list; since Java 8, at <strong>8 entries</strong> in a bucket the list becomes a <strong>red-black tree</strong> (O(n) → O(log n)).</li><li>Defaults: <strong><code>initialCapacity</code> = 16</strong>, <strong><code>loadFactor</code> = 0.75</strong> → 12 elements before a resize.</li><li><code>ArrayList</code> beats <code>LinkedList</code> <strong>in most real cases</strong> — <code>System.arraycopy()</code> outruns allocating <code>Node</code> objects.</li><li><strong>A <code>HashMap</code> key must be immutable.</strong> Changing a field that feeds <code>hashCode()</code> loses the entry.</li><li>A <strong>fail-fast</strong> iterator throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>; a <strong>fail-safe</strong> one iterates over a copy.</li></ul></blockquote>\n<h2>The type hierarchy</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Interface</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Iterable</code></td><td>anything you can walk with a for-each loop</td></tr><tr><td><code>Collection</code></td><td>extends <code>Iterable</code>; adds <code>add</code>, <code>remove</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>size</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>List</code></td><td>an <strong>ordered</strong> collection, accessible by index</td></tr><tr><td><code>Set</code></td><td>an <strong>unordered</strong> collection of <strong>distinct</strong> elements (the mathematical notion)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Queue</code></td><td>a collection with insert/remove/examine methods — a buffer before processing</td></tr><tr><td><code>Map</code></td><td><strong>does not extend <code>Collection</code></strong> — key→value pairs, unique keys, at most one value per key</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Why isn't <code>Map</code> a <code>Collection</code>?</strong> A deliberate design choice — collections and mappings cannot sensibly be gathered under one abstraction.</p></blockquote>\n<h2><code>ArrayList</code> versus <code>LinkedList</code></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>ArrayList</code></th><th><code>LinkedList</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Structure</td><td>an auto-resizing array</td><td>a <strong>doubly-linked</strong> list of <code>Node</code> objects</td></tr><tr><td>Index access</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Insert in the middle</td><td>O(n) — shift the rest</td><td>O(1) <em>once you are there</em></td></tr><tr><td>Memory overhead</td><td>small</td><td><strong>large</strong> — every element is a separate <code>Node</code> with two references</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>The conclusion interviewers are after:</strong> <code>ArrayList</code> wins <strong>almost always</strong>. Its element shifting is O(n), but it is implemented as a <strong>very fast <code>System.arraycopy()</code></strong>. <code>LinkedList</code> offers O(1) insertion but pays for it with a <code>Node</code> allocation and several reference updates — often slower in practice.</p>\n<h2>The <code>Set</code> family</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>HashSet</code></th><th><code>LinkedHashSet</code></th><th><code>TreeSet</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Backed by</td><td><code>HashMap</code></td><td><code>LinkedHashMap</code></td><td><code>TreeMap</code> (red-black tree)</td></tr><tr><td>Order</td><td><strong>none</strong> — iteration is &quot;shuffled&quot;</td><td><strong>insertion order</strong></td><td><strong>sorted</strong> by <code>Comparator</code> / natural order</td></tr><tr><td>Complexity</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(1) + list overhead</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td>Extra</td><td></td><td></td><td>implements <code>NavigableSet</code> — <code>first()</code>, <code>ceiling()</code>, <code>headSet()</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>The <code>Map</code> family</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Order</th><th>Thread-safe</th><th>Complexity</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>HashMap</code></td><td>none</td><td>❌</td><td>O(1)</td><td>the default choice</td></tr><tr><td><code>LinkedHashMap</code></td><td><strong>insertion</strong> (or access)</td><td>❌</td><td>O(1) + overhead</td><td>the basis for an LRU cache</td></tr><tr><td><code>TreeMap</code></td><td><strong>sorted</strong></td><td>❌</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>red-black tree</td></tr><tr><td><code>ConcurrentHashMap</code></td><td>none</td><td>✅</td><td>O(1)</td><td>full concurrency of retrievals — <code>get</code> <strong>does not lock</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>Hashtable</code></td><td>none</td><td>✅</td><td>O(1)</td><td><strong>obsolete</strong> — every method is <code>synchronized</code>, blocking even independent reads</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>How <code>HashMap</code> works inside</h2>\n<p>This is the <strong>most-asked</strong> collections question. The full answer:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>An array whose size is a power of two.</strong> Not an accident — it turns a modulo into bit masking.</li><li><strong>Insertion:</strong> the key's <code>hashCode()</code> is computed (an <code>int</code>). A number of its <strong>lower bits</strong> becomes the array index. That cell is the <strong>bucket</strong>.</li><li><strong>Array index access is O(1)</strong> — the whole point of a hash map.</li><li><strong>Collision:</strong> <code>hashCode</code> is not unique, and even different hash codes can land on the same index. Two keys end up in one bucket.</li><li><strong>Collision resolution:</strong> the bucket holds a <strong>linked list</strong> of entries. Since <strong>Java 8</strong>, when a bucket exceeds the threshold (<strong>8</strong>), the list turns into a <strong>red-black tree</strong> — lookup drops from <strong>O(n) to O(log n)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Telling keys apart:</strong> keys with the same <code>hashCode</code> but not equal coexist in the bucket. <code>HashMap</code> distinguishes them with <strong><code>equals()</code></strong>.</li><li><strong>Retrieval:</strong> compute <code>hashCode</code> → find the bucket → traverse the list/tree → call <code>equals()</code> → return the match.</li><li><strong>Resizing:</strong> when the array fills, it <strong>doubles</strong>. That triggers <strong>rehashing</strong> — rebuilding the internal structures, an expensive operation.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Complexity:</strong> O(1) for <code>put</code> and <code>get</code>. Worst case — everything in one bucket — degrades to <strong>O(log n)</strong> (after treeification). The cure: a good hash function with a <strong>uniform distribution</strong>.</p>\n<h3><code>initialCapacity</code> and <code>loadFactor</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>initialCapacity</code></td><td><strong>16</strong></td><td>the internal array size, <strong>rounded up to a power of two</strong> (ask for 10, you get 16)</td></tr><tr><td><code>loadFactor</code></td><td><strong>0.75</strong></td><td>the <strong>maximum</strong> ratio of elements to buckets; exceeding it triggers a resize</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Example: a <code>HashMap</code> with 16 buckets holding 12 elements has a load factor of 12/16 = 0.75 → <strong>the next insert resizes it</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote><p>Picking a sensible <code>initialCapacity</code> is not trivial. Guava offers <code>Maps.newHashMapWithExpectedSize()</code> and <code>Sets.newHashSetWithExpectedSize()</code>, which do the arithmetic for you. The same applies to <code>HashSet</code> — internally it is a <code>HashMap</code>.</p></blockquote>\n<h2><code>HashMap</code> and concurrency</h2>\n<p><strong>Why it is not safe:</strong> no synchronisation. Concurrent modification can produce inconsistent data, corrupted entries, and in older versions even an <strong>infinite loop</strong> during rehashing.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">// 1. A wrapper — synchronises EVERY access, so it scales poorly\nMap&lt;String, Integer&gt; map = Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap&lt;&gt;());\n\n// 2. ConcurrentHashMap — the right choice under real concurrency\nMap&lt;String, Integer&gt; chm = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();</code></pre>\n<p><code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> locks <strong>only parts</strong> of the map (lock striping in Java 7; since Java 8, fine-grained synchronisation with <strong>CAS</strong> operations). It exposes atomic <code>computeIfAbsent</code>, <code>putIfAbsent</code>, <code>merge</code>.</p>\n<h2><code>null</code> in maps</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>null</code> key</th><th><code>null</code> value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>HashMap</code></td><td><strong>one</strong> — always lands in bucket 0, bypassing hashing</td><td>many</td></tr><tr><td><code>Hashtable</code></td><td>❌ <code>NullPointerException</code></td><td>❌ <code>NullPointerException</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ConcurrentHashMap</code></td><td>❌</td><td>❌</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Useful <code>Map</code> methods</h2>\n<h3><code>putIfAbsent</code> versus <code>computeIfAbsent</code></h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">map.putIfAbsent(&quot;key&quot;, new ArrayList&lt;&gt;());          // the value is computed ALWAYS\nmap.computeIfAbsent(&quot;key&quot;, k -&gt; new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()); // the function runs ONLY if the key is absent</code></pre>\n<p>When building the value is expensive, <strong><code>computeIfAbsent</code> is clearly more efficient</strong> — <code>putIfAbsent</code> needs a precomputed value, so it builds one even when it will not be used.</p>\n<h3><code>merge</code></h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">map.merge(&quot;key&quot;, 1, Integer::sum);   // add 1 to the existing value, or insert 1</code></pre>\n<ul><li><strong>When to use:</strong> counting occurrences (word frequencies), accumulating values — one atomic operation instead of conditionals.</li><li><strong>When not to:</strong> if the remapping function returns <code>null</code>, the <strong>key is removed from the map</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2>Enum collections</h2>\n<p><strong>Always</strong> use <code>EnumSet</code> and <code>EnumMap</code> when the keys are enums.</p>\n<ul><li><strong><code>EnumSet</code></strong> is simply a <strong>bit vector</strong> — a one at the position matching <code>ordinal()</code>. Membership check = check a bit.</li><li><strong><code>EnumMap</code></strong> is an <strong>array</strong> indexed by <code>ordinal()</code>. No hash codes, no collision resolution.</li></ul>\n<h2>Fail-fast versus fail-safe iterators</h2>\n<p><strong>Concurrent modification</strong> is not only a change from another thread — it also covers a change from the same thread via another iterator or directly on the collection.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Fail-fast</th><th>Fail-safe</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who</td><td><code>HashMap</code>, <code>ArrayList</code>, non-synchronized collections</td><td><code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>, <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code></td></tr><tr><td>Behaviour</td><td>iterates the <strong>original</strong> structure and throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> as soon as it detects a change</td><td>iterates over a <strong>copy</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Downside</td><td>the exception</td><td>heavy memory use + possibly <strong>stale</strong> data</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>The classic trap:</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">// ❌ ConcurrentModificationException\nfor (Map.Entry&lt;Integer, String&gt; entry : map.entrySet()) {\n    if (entry.getKey().equals(2)) {\n        map.remove(entry.getKey());\n    }\n}\n\n// ✅ Iterator.remove() — the only safe way to remove while iterating\nIterator&lt;Map.Entry&lt;Integer, String&gt;&gt; it = map.entrySet().iterator();\nwhile (it.hasNext()) {\n    Map.Entry&lt;Integer, String&gt; entry = it.next();\n    if (entry.getKey().equals(2)) {\n        it.remove();\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<h2><code>Comparable</code> versus <code>Comparator</code></h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>Comparable</code></th><th><code>Comparator</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Where</td><td><strong>inside</strong> the sorted object's class</td><td>a <strong>separate</strong> class or lambda</td></tr><tr><td>Method</td><td><code>compareTo(T other)</code></td><td><code>compare(T a, T b)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Role</td><td>the <strong>natural order</strong></td><td>an <strong>alternative</strong> order</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td><code>Integer</code>, <code>Long</code>, <code>String</code> (lexicographic)</td><td>sorting descending</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>Comparable</code> lets you sort with <code>Collections.sort()</code> and sets the iteration order in <code>SortedSet</code> / <code>SortedMap</code>.</p>\n<p><code>Comparator</code> is a <strong>functional interface</strong>, so a lambda will do:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">List&lt;Integer&gt; list = Arrays.asList(5, 2, 3, 4, 1);\n\nCollections.sort(list);                      // natural order → first is 1\nCollections.sort(list, (a, b) -&gt; b - a);     // descending    → first is 5</code></pre>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Caution:</strong> <code>(a, b) -&gt; b - a</code> overflows at extreme <code>int</code> values. In production: <code>Comparator.reverseOrder()</code> or <code>Comparator.comparingInt(...).reversed()</code>.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>Complexity table</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th><code>ArrayList</code></th><th><code>LinkedList</code></th><th><code>HashMap</code>/<code>HashSet</code></th><th><code>TreeMap</code>/<code>TreeSet</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>get</code> by index</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(n)</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>get</code> by key</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>add</code> at the end</td><td>O(1)*</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>add</code> / <code>remove</code> in the middle</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(1)**</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>contains</code></td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n)</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong></td><td>O(log n)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>\\<em> amortised — the array occasionally grows \\</em>* once the node is found; reaching it is O(n)</p>\n<h2>Interview questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Q: How does <code>HashMap</code> work internally?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> An array of buckets sized to a power of two. The key's <code>hashCode()</code> picks the index, collisions are chained in the bucket, and since Java 8 the chain becomes a red-black tree at 8 entries. Keys within a bucket are told apart by <code>equals()</code>. Exceeding the <code>loadFactor</code> doubles the array and triggers rehashing.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: <code>ArrayList</code> or <code>LinkedList</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> In practice almost always <code>ArrayList</code>. Its O(n) shifting is <code>System.arraycopy()</code>, a very fast memory operation, whereas <code>LinkedList</code>'s O(1) requires allocating a <code>Node</code> and updating references. <code>LinkedList</code> earns its place on very large lists with heavy insertion at varying positions.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: What happens when a <code>HashMap</code> key is mutable?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Once a field feeding <code>hashCode()</code> changes, the entry <strong>disappears</strong> — the map computes a new hash, goes to a different bucket, and finds nothing. Keys must be immutable, or at least have <code>final</code> fields in <code>hashCode()</code>.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">Map&lt;Employee, String&gt; map = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();\nEmployee emp = new Employee(1, &quot;John&quot;);\nmap.put(emp, &quot;Engineer&quot;);\nemp.setId(2);              // a field used by hashCode() changed\nmap.get(emp);              // null — the entry is &quot;lost&quot;</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Q: <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> versus <code>Hashtable</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Both are thread-safe, but <code>Hashtable</code> synchronises <strong>every</strong> method, blocking even independent reads. <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> locks only parts of the structure and allows fully concurrent <code>get</code>. <code>Hashtable</code> is considered obsolete.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Fail-fast versus fail-safe?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Fail-fast iterates the original structure and throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> when it detects a modification. Fail-safe works on a copy — it throws nothing, but costs memory and may show stale data.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: How would you design an LRU cache in Java?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> A <code>LinkedHashMap</code> constructed with <code>accessOrder = true</code> and an overridden <code>removeEldestEntry()</code>. The linked list maintains access order, and the overridden method evicts the oldest entry once capacity is exceeded.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>A mutable key in <code>HashMap</code>/<code>HashSet</code></strong> — the entry is lost.</li><li><strong>Overriding <code>equals()</code> without <code>hashCode()</code></strong> — an object put in a map becomes unfindable.</li><li><strong>Modifying a collection inside a for-each loop</strong> → <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>. Use <code>Iterator.remove()</code>.</li><li><strong><code>Arrays.asList()</code> returns a fixed-size list</strong> — <code>add()</code> throws <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code>.</li><li><strong><code>merge</code> with a function returning <code>null</code></strong> removes the key.</li><li><strong><code>Collections.synchronizedMap</code> is not enough for iteration</strong> — that must be wrapped in <code>synchronized</code> yourself.</li></ul>\n<h2>Related</h2>\n<p><span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Type_System_And_Language_Building_Blocks_EN</span> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Java_8_Lambdas_Streams_Functional_Interfaces_EN</span> · <a href=\"#/Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_EN\">Multithreading — threads, executors, deadlocks</a> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Collections_Framework_Cheatsheet_PL</span></p>"},"Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_EN":{"title":"Multithreading — threads, executors, deadlocks","category":"Java/Concurrency","date":"2026-11-04","tags":[],"minutes":7,"lead":"","html":"<blockquote><p><strong>TL;DR</strong></p><ul><li><code>synchronized</code> gives <strong>atomicity + visibility</strong>. <code>volatile</code> gives <strong>visibility only</strong>.</li><li><code>volatile int count; count++</code> <strong>is still broken</strong> — you need <code>AtomicInteger</code> or <code>synchronized</code>.</li><li><code>sleep()</code> <strong>keeps the lock</strong>, <code>wait()</code> <strong>releases it</strong>. Always call <code>wait()</code> in a <code>while</code> loop, never an <code>if</code>.</li><li><code>parallelStream()</code> uses the <strong>common</strong> ForkJoin pool — one blocking I/O call stalls parallel streams across the <strong>whole</strong> application.</li><li><strong>Deadlock</strong> = threads are stuck (<code>BLOCKED</code>). <strong>Livelock</strong> = threads are running (<code>RUNNABLE</code>) but nothing progresses.</li><li><strong>Never swallow <code>InterruptedException</code>.</strong></li></ul></blockquote>\n<h2><code>synchronized</code> — access control</h2>\n<p>Guarantees that only <strong>one thread at a time</strong> executes a given piece of code.</p>\n<p><strong>Analogy:</strong> the toilet on a train. The toilet is the shared resource, the people in the corridor are the threads, <code>synchronized</code> is the lock on the door. Thread A enters and locks it. Thread B waits. A leaves and unlocks. Only now can B go in.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">public class Counter {\n    private int count = 0;\n\n    // one thread at a time\n    public synchronized void increment() {\n        count++;\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Without <code>synchronized</code>, two threads doing <code>count++</code> at once can overwrite each other — instead of 2 you get 1. <code>count++</code> is really <strong>three</strong> operations: read, increment, write.</p>\n<h2><code>volatile</code> — visibility</h2>\n<p>Guarantees that a change to the variable is <strong>immediately visible</strong> to other threads.</p>\n<p><strong>Analogy:</strong> main memory (RAM) is the big noticeboard in the corridor; the CPU cache is each employee's private notepad at their desk.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Without <code>volatile</code>:</strong> Employee A changes the status to &quot;DONE&quot; but writes it only in their notepad. Employee B looks at the noticeboard and still sees &quot;IN PROGRESS&quot; — working on stale data.</li><li><strong>With <code>volatile</code>:</strong> A is forbidden from using the notepad. Every change goes straight to the board.</li></ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">public class Worker extends Thread {\n    private volatile boolean running = true;   // the change is seen immediately\n\n    public void run() {\n        while (running) { /* work */ }\n    }\n\n    public void stopWork() {\n        running = false;   // writes straight to main memory\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<h3>The key difference — an interview question</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>synchronized</code></th><th><code>volatile</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Atomicity</td><td>✅</td><td>❌</td></tr><tr><td>Visibility</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td></tr><tr><td>Ordering (reordering)</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td><strong>heavy</strong></td><td><strong>light</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Will <code>volatile</code> fix <code>i++</code>?</strong> <strong>No.</strong> <code>volatile</code> makes threads see the current state, but does not stop them modifying it at the same moment. For a counter you need <code>synchronized</code> or <code>AtomicInteger</code>.</p></blockquote>\n<h2><code>synchronized</code> versus <code>AtomicInteger</code></h2>\n<blockquote><p><code>synchronized</code> is a <strong>blocking (pessimistic)</strong> mechanism that <strong>parks threads</strong>, forcing an expensive <strong>context switch</strong>. <code>AtomicInteger</code> is <strong>non-blocking</strong> and rests on the processor's <strong>CAS</strong> (<em>Compare-And-Swap</em>) instruction — much faster, because threads are not parked but retry in a loop. <strong>The difference in scope:</strong> atomics protect a <strong>single variable</strong>; <code>synchronized</code> lets you execute a <strong>whole block</strong> over several variables atomically.</p></blockquote>\n<h3>The evolution of counters — performance</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>How it works</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>synchronized</code> / <code>ReentrantLock</code></td><td>pessimistic locking</td><td><strong>slowest</strong> under heavy contention</td></tr><tr><td><code>AtomicInteger</code> / <code>AtomicLong</code></td><td>CAS, non-blocking</td><td>fast; <strong>downside:</strong> under heavy load threads spin in a loop, burning CPU</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>LongAdder</code></strong> (Java 8+)</td><td>an <strong>array of cells</strong> — different threads write to different cells, summed on demand</td><td><strong>the default choice with very many writers</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>The Java Memory Model and <code>happens-before</code></h2>\n<p><strong><code>happens-before</code></strong> is the guarantee that the results of operation A are visible to operation B.</p>\n<p>The core rules:</p>\n<ul><li>A write to a <code>volatile</code> variable <strong>happens-before</strong> a read of it.</li><li>Releasing a lock (<em>unlock</em>) <strong>happens-before</strong> acquiring it again (<em>lock</em>).</li><li><code>Thread.start()</code> <strong>happens-before</strong> every action in that thread.</li><li>Every action in a thread <strong>happens-before</strong> <code>join()</code> on it returns.</li></ul>\n<p>What <code>volatile</code> provides:</p>\n<ul><li>✅ <strong>visibility</strong> — bypasses the CPU cache,</li><li>✅ <strong>ordering</strong> — prevents instruction reordering by the compiler and CPU,</li><li>❌ <strong>atomicity</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>More on the JMM and JIT optimisations → <a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_EN\">JIT, the Java Memory Model, monitoring and observability</a>.</p>\n<h2>Coordination tools — <code>java.util.concurrent</code></h2>\n<p><code>synchronized</code> is not the whole story. These four classes show up in senior interviews:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Behaviour</th><th>Scenario</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>CountDownLatch</code></td><td>waits until a counter hits zero; <strong>single use</strong></td><td>&quot;wait for 5 independent services (cache, DB, MQ) to start before launching the main server&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><code>CyclicBarrier</code></td><td>waits until N threads reach a rendezvous point; <strong>reusable</strong></td><td>&quot;map-reduce: 4 threads compute partial results, wait for each other, merge, and move to the next round&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><code>Semaphore</code></td><td>caps the <strong>number</strong> of threads accessing a resource</td><td>&quot;rate limiting — the external API allows 10 concurrent connections, set the semaphore to 10&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><code>Phaser</code></td><td>a more flexible barrier with a <strong>dynamic</strong> participant count</td><td>asked less often, but worth knowing as the &quot;modern barrier&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Fork/Join and work stealing</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>The problem with a classic pool:</strong> when one thread finishes while another still has 100 tasks, the first sits idle.</li><li><strong>The fix:</strong> in a <code>ForkJoinPool</code> <strong>every thread has its own queue</strong> (<code>Deque</code>). When it empties, it <strong>steals a task from the tail</strong> of a busy thread's queue.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>The senior question: why can <code>parallelStream()</code> be dangerous?</strong> Because by default it uses <code>ForkJoinPool.commonPool()</code>, which is <strong>shared by the whole JVM</strong>. Block its threads (on I/O, say) and you stall <strong>every</strong> parallel stream in the application.</p>\n<h2>Thread lifecycle and <code>Object</code> methods</h2>\n<h3><code>wait()</code> versus <code>sleep()</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>sleep()</code></th><th><code>wait()</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Class</td><td><code>Thread</code></td><td><strong><code>Object</code></strong></td></tr><tr><td>Lock</td><td><strong>keeps</strong> it</td><td><strong>releases</strong> the monitor</td></tr><tr><td>Requires</td><td>nothing</td><td>must be inside a <code>synchronized</code> block</td></tr><tr><td>Wake-up</td><td>after the timeout</td><td>via <code>notify()</code> / <code>notifyAll()</code> / timeout</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Why <code>wait()</code> in a <code>while</code>, not an <code>if</code>?</h3>\n<p>Because of <strong>spurious wakeups</strong> — the operating system may wake a thread for no reason. After waking you <strong>must re-check</strong> that the condition actually holds.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">synchronized (lock) {\n    while (!condition) {   // NOT 'if'\n        lock.wait();\n    }\n    // process\n}</code></pre>\n<h3><code>join()</code> and <code>InterruptedException</code></h3>\n<p><code>Thread.join()</code> lets one thread wait for another to finish. It throws <code>InterruptedException</code>.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Senior rule: never swallow <code>InterruptedException</code>.</strong> Either rethrow it, or restore the flag:</p><pre><code class=\"language-java\">catch (InterruptedException e) {\n    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();   // upper layers must know\n    throw new IllegalStateException(e);\n}</code></pre></blockquote>\n<h2>Interview questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Q: What is a livelock and how does it differ from a deadlock?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> In a <strong>deadlock</strong> threads are stuck — state <code>BLOCKED</code>. In a <strong>livelock</strong> they are active (<code>RUNNABLE</code>), continually changing state in reaction to each other while nothing progresses. <strong>Analogy:</strong> two people in a narrow corridor trying to pass — both step left, then both step right, forever. It often arises from a badly implemented deadlock-recovery mechanism.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Can you start a thread twice?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> No. Calling <code>start()</code> on a thread that has already started, or finished, throws <code>IllegalThreadStateException</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: User thread versus daemon thread?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> The JVM lives as long as <strong>at least one User thread</strong> is running. <strong>Daemon</strong> threads (the GC, for instance) are killed automatically once all User threads finish. Mark a background helper that should not block shutdown as a daemon.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: What is a ThreadLocal memory leak?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Thread pools (in Tomcat, say) <strong>reuse</strong> threads. If you store a large object in a <code>ThreadLocal</code> and never call <code>remove()</code> at the end of the request, the object stays <strong>pinned to the thread</strong> and the GC cannot reclaim it, even though the request is over. Across thousands of requests this is a real leak. → <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_EN</span></p>\n<p><strong>Q: When <code>LongAdder</code> instead of <code>AtomicLong</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> When you have <strong>very many writers</strong>. Under contention <code>AtomicLong</code> wastes CPU spinning in the CAS loop; <code>LongAdder</code> spreads writes across an array of cells and sums only on demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: How does <code>Runnable</code> differ from <code>Callable</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> <code>Runnable.run()</code> returns nothing and cannot throw a checked exception. <code>Callable.call()</code> <strong>returns a value</strong> and <strong>may throw</strong>; submitted to an <code>ExecutorService</code> it yields a <code>Future</code> from which you collect the result or the exception wrapped in <code>ExecutionException</code>.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls</h2>\n<ul><li><strong><code>volatile</code> on a counter</strong> — the classic false comfort. It gives no atomicity.</li><li><strong><code>wait()</code> inside an <code>if</code></strong> instead of a <code>while</code> — a spurious wakeup lets code run with the condition unmet.</li><li><strong>Blocking I/O inside <code>parallelStream()</code></strong> — poisons the common ForkJoin pool for the whole JVM.</li><li><strong><code>ThreadLocal</code> without <code>remove()</code></strong> in a thread pool — a memory leak.</li><li><strong>A swallowed <code>InterruptedException</code></strong> — the thread cannot be stopped and application shutdown hangs.</li><li><strong>Acquiring locks in a different order</strong> in two methods — the shortest path to a deadlock. Establish a global lock ordering.</li><li><strong><code>synchronized</code> on an <code>Integer</code>/<code>String</code></strong> — those objects are cached and shared, so you are locking something entirely different from what you think.</li></ul>\n<h2>Related</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/Java/JVM_Internals/JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_EN\">JIT, the Java Memory Model, monitoring and observability</a> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_EN</span> · <a href=\"#/Java/Collections/Collections_Framework_Cheatsheet_EN\">Collections Framework — cheatsheet</a> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Java_8_Lambdas_Streams_Functional_Interfaces_EN</span> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_PL</span></p>"},"Java/JVM_Internals/JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_EN":{"title":"JIT, the Java Memory Model, monitoring and observability","category":"Java/JVM_Internals","date":"2026-11-04","tags":[],"minutes":8,"lead":"","html":"<blockquote><p><strong>TL;DR</strong></p><ul><li>The JIT makes Java a <strong>hybrid</strong>: fast startup from the interpreter, C++-class throughput once hot paths are compiled.</li><li><strong>Inlining is the single most important JIT optimisation</strong> — it removes call overhead and unlocks further optimisations.</li><li><strong>Deoptimisation</strong> is Java's advantage over static compilers: the JVM can undo a speculation once the assumption stops holding.</li><li><strong>Metrics are data; observability is a property of the system.</strong> Metrics answer &quot;is it working&quot;; observability answers &quot;why isn't it&quot;.</li><li>The distinction that wins interviews: <strong>cardinality</strong>.</li></ul></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. JIT — the just-in-time compiler</h2>\n<h3>What it is</h3>\n<p>A HotSpot JVM mechanism that converts bytecode into machine code <strong>at runtime</strong>, targeted at the actual CPU. It is why Java — despite starting interpreted — reaches throughput close to C++.</p>\n<h3>Code lifecycle</h3>\n<pre><code>1. INTERPRETATION       the JVM reads bytecode instruction by instruction\n   ├─ upside:   fast startup, no waiting for compilation\n   └─ downside: slow execution over a long run\n            │\n            ▼\n2. PROFILING            the JVM counts method invocations and loop iterations,\n                        looking for hot spots\n            │\n            ▼\n3. COMPILATION          past a threshold (on the order of 10 000 calls)\n                        the JIT compiles the method to assembly and optimises\n            │\n            ▼\n4. CODE CACHE           compiled native code goes into a dedicated memory area;\n                        subsequent calls bypass the interpreter</code></pre>\n<h3>Key optimisations</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Optimisation</th><th>What it does</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Inlining</strong></td><td>pastes the body of a small method <code>B()</code> directly into <code>A()</code> instead of performing a jump</td><td><strong>the most important optimisation in Java</strong> — it removes call overhead and, more importantly, lets the JIT optimise the merged code as one unit</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dead code elimination</strong></td><td>removes code that can never run (<code>if (false) {...}</code>)</td><td>the machine code carries no dead branches</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Loop unrolling</strong></td><td>unrolls loops, reducing condition checks</td><td>fewer jumps, better branch prediction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Tiered compilation</h3>\n<p>A modern JVM runs <strong>two compilers at once</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Compiler</th><th>Compile speed</th><th>Code quality</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>C1</strong> (client)</td><td>very fast</td><td>simple optimisations</td><td>quick speed-up right after startup</td></tr><tr><td><strong>C2</strong> (server)</td><td>slow</td><td>aggressive, deep optimisations</td><td>maximum throughput for genuinely hot methods</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>This is exactly what combines <strong>fast startup</strong> with <strong>peak long-run throughput</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Deoptimisation — the safety net</h3>\n<p>The JIT is <strong>speculative</strong>. It may assume <em>&quot;this method never receives <code>null</code>&quot;</em> and compile without the check.</p>\n<p>When the assumption breaks, <strong>deoptimisation</strong> happens: the JVM discards the fast native code, falls back to the interpreter, and recompiles — this time accounting for the new conditions.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>This is the edge over a static compiler.</strong> C++ cannot revisit a decision made at build time. The JIT optimises for the <strong>traffic the application actually sees</strong>, not for compile-time assumptions.</p></blockquote>\n<h3>Summary for a recruiter</h3>\n<blockquote><p><em>&quot;The JIT is a hybrid mechanism. The application starts interpreted so it boots fast, while the JVM profiles the code in the background looking for hot spots. Once a method crosses the invocation threshold it is compiled to native code with inlining and dead-code elimination. Tiered compilation — C1 first, then C2 — combines fast startup with maximum throughput. And because the JIT runs at runtime, it can optimise better than a static compiler, reacting to real traffic.&quot;</em></p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. The Java Memory Model — what it actually is</h2>\n<p><strong>The JMM is not a map of memory regions.</strong> It is the set of rules governing <strong>when a write by one thread becomes visible to another</strong>, and in what order operations may be observed.</p>\n<p>Confusing the JMM with memory areas (heap, stack, metaspace) is the most common mistake in this part of an interview. Memory areas are covered in <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">JVM_Architecture_And_Classloaders_EN</span>; visibility and <code>happens-before</code> in <a href=\"#/Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_EN\">Multithreading — threads, executors, deadlocks</a>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>3. Observability — the three pillars</h2>\n<p>The modern framing is not &quot;monitoring&quot; but <strong>observability</strong>, resting on three pillars: <strong>metrics, logs, traces</strong>.</p>\n<h3>The standard stack (open source / cloud native)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Role</th><th>Nuance worth knowing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Prometheus</strong></td><td>time-series database, metrics</td><td>operates on a <strong>PULL</strong> model — it scrapes your services every few seconds from <code>/actuator/prometheus</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>AlertManager</strong></td><td>notifications</td><td>a Prometheus component; fires when e.g. error rate &gt; 5 %</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Grafana</strong></td><td>visualisation</td><td><em>single pane of glass</em> — one dashboard combining Prometheus metrics with logs from Loki/Elasticsearch</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Micrometer</strong></td><td>Java abstraction layer</td><td>&quot;SLF4J for metrics&quot;: you write <code>Timer.record()</code> and Micrometer translates it for Prometheus, Datadog or Dynatrace — <strong>switching monitoring systems is a configuration change, not a code change</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Trap:</strong> without <code>spring-boot-starter-actuator</code>, Micrometer <strong>exposes no endpoints</strong>. Metrics are collected and nobody can see them.</p></blockquote>\n<h3>Distributed tracing</h3>\n<p>Metrics tell you <em>it is slow</em>. Logs tell you <em>something blew up</em>. Only tracing tells you <strong>where those 5 seconds went</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Trace ID</strong></td><td>identifier of the whole request, carried across every service</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Span ID</strong></td><td>identifier of a single operation inside one service</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Tools: Zipkin, Jaeger, OpenTelemetry. The output is a Gantt chart for a single request — <code>Service A (10 ms) → Service B (4900 ms) → DB</code>. The culprit is immediately visible.</p>\n<h3>Enterprise-class APM</h3>\n<p>AppDynamics, Dynatrace and New Relic work through <strong>bytecode instrumentation</strong> — you attach an agent to the process (<code>-javaagent:agent.jar</code>) and it sees everything <strong>without a line of code changing</strong>.</p>\n<p>What you get: automatic topology discovery, visibility down to the individual SQL statement, ML-based anomaly detection.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>4. Metrics versus observability — the senior-level distinction</h2>\n<p>Many candidates use the terms interchangeably. That is a mistake.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Metrics (monitoring)</th><th>Observability</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Answers</strong></td><td>&quot;Is the system up?&quot;, &quot;What is happening?&quot;</td><td><strong>&quot;WHY is this happening?&quot;</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nature of data</strong></td><td>aggregated numbers</td><td>metrics + logs + traces together</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td><em>known unknowns</em> — you monitor what you anticipated</td><td><em>unknown unknowns</em> — failures nobody planned for</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Storage cost</strong></td><td>low</td><td>higher</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The equation for a recruiter:</strong> metrics are a <strong>subset</strong> of observability. With metrics alone you do not have observability, because you lack context.</p></blockquote>\n<h3>Cardinality — the argument that wins interviews</h3>\n<p><strong>Metrics cannot handle high cardinality.</strong> You cannot tag <code>response_time</code> with a user id — at a million users Prometheus explodes in time-series count. So metrics only ever say <em>&quot;the average is 200 ms&quot;</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Genuine observability tools permit high cardinality</strong> — you can ask for the requests of one specific user. That lets you debug <strong>individual</strong> problems, not just systemic ones.</p>\n<h3>The narrative to tell</h3>\n<p><strong>Metrics-led:</strong> an alert wakes me at 03:00. Grafana shows <code>http_errors_total</code> jumped to 15 %. I know there is a problem; I do not know what it is. I start guessing: the database? the new deploy?</p>\n<p><strong>Observability-led:</strong> the alert arrives and I can slice the data. Filtering shows a correlation — 100 % of errors come from one endpoint and affect users with a particular product category in the basket. Traces show a <code>NullPointerException</code> in the inventory service for that category. <strong>I know why in 30 seconds, without guessing.</strong></p>\n<hr>\n<h2>5. Kibana (ELK) versus Splunk</h2>\n<p>Not the same thing, but direct competitors. For a senior, two differences matter: <strong>architecture</strong> and <strong>when the schema is applied</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Kibana (ELK)</th><th>Splunk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Type</strong></td><td>a UI for Elasticsearch, nothing more</td><td>a complete end-to-end platform</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Assembly</strong></td><td>you assemble the pieces: Elasticsearch + Logstash/Beats + Kibana</td><td><em>enterprise ready out of the box</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Schema</strong></td><td><strong>schema on write</strong> — parse before indexing</td><td><strong>schema on read</strong> — ingest raw, apply structure at query time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Query language</strong></td><td>KQL / Lucene — <code>status:500 AND env:prod</code></td><td>SPL — `index=main \\</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cost model</strong></td><td>infrastructure plus engineering time</td><td>licensed per GB/day or vCPU — <strong>one of the most expensive tools on the market</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Strength</strong></td><td>dashboards, cloud-native observability</td><td>ad-hoc investigation, SIEM, banking</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Consequences of the schema choice</h3>\n<p><strong>Schema on write (Elasticsearch):</strong> fast search, because the data is already structured. But if the log format changes in the application and the parser does not, <strong>you lose data or cannot find it</strong>. Changing a field definition requires reindexing.</p>\n<p><strong>Schema on read (Splunk):</strong> enormous flexibility — you can analyse data whose structure you did not know in advance, which is ideal for investigating unfamiliar ground. The price is CPU load, because parsing happens on <strong>every</strong> query.</p>\n<blockquote><p>SPL lets you program in the search bar — join transactions, compute time deltas, correlate in a single query. Here Splunk clearly outclasses Kibana.</p></blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2>Interview questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Q: Is Java interpreted or compiled?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Both. It starts interpreted for fast startup, and hot methods are JIT-compiled to native code. Tiered compilation combines the two — C1 fast, C2 aggressive.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: What is the most important JIT optimisation?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Inlining. It removes method-call overhead, but more importantly it lets the JIT optimise the merged code as a single unit.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: What happens when a JIT speculation turns out to be wrong?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Deoptimisation — fall back to the interpreter and recompile under the new assumptions. A static compiler cannot do this.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: How do metrics differ from observability?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Metrics are data, observability is a property of the system. Metrics cover known unknowns and are low-cardinality; observability combines metrics, logs and traces, tolerates high cardinality, and lets you diagnose cases nobody anticipated.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Why not tag a metric with a user id?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Cardinality explosion. Every distinct label value is a separate time series — at a million users the metrics store stops working.</p>\n<h2>Pitfalls</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>A microbenchmark without warm-up measures the interpreter, not the JIT.</strong> The first few thousand iterations are unrepresentative — hence JMH and its warm-up phase.</li><li><strong>The code cache is finite.</strong> Once full, the JIT stops compiling and throughput drops — silently.</li><li><strong>The JMM is not memory areas.</strong> It is the inter-thread visibility rule set.</li><li><strong>No actuator means no metrics</strong>, however correctly Micrometer is configured.</li></ul>\n<h2>Related</h2>\n<p><span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">JVM_Architecture_And_Classloaders_EN</span> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_EN</span> · <a href=\"#/Java/Concurrency/Multithreading_Executors_Deadlocks_EN\">Multithreading — threads, executors, deadlocks</a> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">JIT_JMM_GC_Monitoring_PL</span></p>"},"Java/Language_Fundamentals/Exception_Handling_And_IO_EN":{"title":"Exception handling and I/O operations","category":"Java/Language_Fundamentals","date":"2026-11-04","tags":[],"minutes":8,"lead":"","html":"<blockquote><p><strong>TL;DR</strong></p><ul><li>An <strong>exception</strong> is an event you can recover from. An <strong><code>Error</code></strong> is a situation you usually cannot.</li><li><strong>Checked</strong> must be handled or declared. <strong>Unchecked</strong> (<code>RuntimeException</code>, <code>Error</code> and subclasses) need neither.</li><li><code>throw</code> <strong>throws</strong> an exception; <code>throws</code> <strong>declares</strong> that a method may throw one.</li><li><code>finally</code> runs almost always — <strong>except</strong> after <code>System.exit()</code> or a fatal JVM failure.</li><li><strong><code>try-with-resources</code> (Java 7) replaces <code>finally</code> for closing resources.</strong> Make it your default.</li></ul></blockquote>\n<h2>Mental model</h2>\n<p>Exceptions are a <strong>separate communication channel</strong> alongside the return value. Ordinary code flows down the main course; when something goes wrong, control jumps onto a side track to the nearest matching <code>catch</code> — possibly several stack frames up.</p>\n<p>That is what keeps business logic from being interleaved with error-code checks. It is the main advantage over the classic &quot;return −1&quot;.</p>\n<h2>Key concepts</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>throw</code></td><td><strong>throws</strong> an exception object, interrupting the normal flow</td></tr><tr><td><code>throws</code></td><td><strong>declares</strong> in the signature that a method may throw — forces the caller to deal with it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>checked</strong></td><td>the compiler requires handling or declaration (= <em>compile-time</em>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>unchecked</strong></td><td><code>RuntimeException</code>, <code>Error</code> and their subclasses (= <em>runtime</em>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>stack trace</strong></td><td>the list of classes and methods from application start to the throw site</td></tr><tr><td><strong>chaining</strong></td><td>an exception thrown in response to another, preserving the cause</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Hierarchy: <code>Exception</code> versus <code>Error</code></h2>\n<ul><li><strong><code>Exception</code></strong> — a condition you <strong>can recover from</strong>. The file is missing → ask for another one.</li><li><strong><code>Error</code></strong> — usually an <strong>external</strong> situation you cannot recover from. Everything the JVM throws.</li></ul>\n<p>The common JVM errors:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>OutOfMemoryError</code></td><td>the JVM cannot allocate an object and GC freed nothing → <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_EN</span></td></tr><tr><td><code>StackOverflowError</code></td><td>a thread's stack is exhausted, typically by recursion that goes too deep</td></tr><tr><td><code>ExceptionInInitializerError</code></td><td>an exception during a static initialiser</td></tr><tr><td><code>NoClassDefFoundError</code></td><td>the classloader <strong>could not find</strong> a class definition absent from the classpath</td></tr><tr><td><code>UnsupportedClassVersionError</code></td><td>a <code>.class</code> file built by a newer Java than the one reading it</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<blockquote><p>You <strong>can</strong> catch an <code>Error</code> in a <code>try</code>, but it is <strong>bad practice</strong> — there is no guarantee the program can do anything reliably afterwards.</p></blockquote>\n<h3><code>ClassNotFoundException</code> versus <code>NoClassDefFoundError</code></h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>ClassNotFoundException</code></th><th><code>NoClassDefFoundError</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kind</td><td><strong>checked exception</strong></td><td><strong>Error</strong></td></tr><tr><td>When</td><td>a dynamically loaded class (reflection, <code>Class.forName</code>) does not exist</td><td>the class <strong>was</strong> there at compile time, but the JVM cannot find it at runtime</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>How to handle</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">try {\n    // &quot;guarded&quot; code\n} catch (ExceptionType1 ex) {\n    // ...\n} catch (ExceptionType2 ex) {\n    // ...\n} finally {\n    // runs after the try block exits — exception or not\n}</code></pre>\n<h3>Three ways to handle multiple exceptions</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">// 1. One broad catch — WORKS, but do not do this\ntry { /* ... */ } catch (Exception ex) { /* ... */ }\n\n// 2. Multiple catch blocks — child BEFORE parent, otherwise a compile error\ntry { /* ... */ }\ncatch (FileNotFoundException ex) { /* ... */ }\ncatch (EOFException ex)          { /* ... */ }\n\n// 3. Multi-catch (Java 7) — less duplication\ntry { /* ... */ }\ncatch (FileNotFoundException | EOFException ex) { /* ... */ }</code></pre>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Rule:</strong> catch as <strong>narrowly</strong> as you can. An over-broad handler catches exceptions you never anticipated and turns a failure into quiet, wrong behaviour.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>Exception chaining</h2>\n<p>You throw a new exception <strong>in response</strong> to another while preserving the original cause. Without it you lose the whole history of the problem.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">try {\n    task.readConfigFile();\n} catch (FileNotFoundException ex) {\n    throw new TaskException(&quot;Could not perform task&quot;, ex);   // ex = cause\n}</code></pre>\n<p><code>Throwable.getCause()</code> retrieves the cause set via the constructor or <code>initCause()</code>.</p>\n<h2>Custom exceptions</h2>\n<p>Create your own type when:</p>\n<ul><li>no existing platform type describes the situation,</li><li>you want to give the caller <strong>more context</strong> to react precisely.</li></ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">class MyException extends Exception {\n    public MyException(String message) { super(message); }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Checked or unchecked?</strong> Rule of thumb: if code using your exception <strong>can be expected to recover</strong>, make it checked. Otherwise unchecked. Inherit from the <strong>most specific</strong> matching class; if there is none, from <code>Exception</code>.</p>\n<h2><code>try-with-resources</code> (Java 7)</h2>\n<p>Automatically closes resources implementing <code>AutoCloseable</code> — files, sockets, connections.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(&quot;file.txt&quot;))) {\n    // ...\n} catch (IOException e) {\n    log.error(&quot;Failed to read the file&quot;, e);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Advantages over a hand-written <code>finally</code>:</p>\n<ul><li>The resource is <strong>always</strong> closed, even when the <code>catch</code> itself throws.</li><li>An exception from <code>close()</code> does not <strong>mask</strong> the one from the <code>try</code> block — it goes to the suppressed exceptions (<code>getSuppressed()</code>).</li></ul>\n<blockquote><p>That last point is a real bug in hand-written <code>finally</code> code: an exception thrown in <code>finally</code> <strong>swallows</strong> the one from <code>try</code> or <code>catch</code>.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>Exceptions and inheritance — overriding rules</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Parent method</th><th>What the child method may do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>declares nothing</td><td><strong>no checked</strong>, any unchecked</td></tr><tr><td>declares checked <code>A, B</code></td><td>none, a subset, or <strong>narrower</strong> subtypes; never broader or new ones</td></tr><tr><td>declares unchecked</td><td>any unchecked, even unrelated ones</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">// ✅ OK — fewer exceptions and a narrower scope\nclass Parent {\n    void doSomething()     throws IOException, ParseException { }\n    void doSomethingElse() throws IOException { }\n}\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    void doSomething()     throws IOException { }\n    void doSomethingElse() throws FileNotFoundException, EOFException { }\n}\n\n// ❌ Compile error — IOException is BROADER than FileNotFoundException\nclass P { void doSomething() throws FileNotFoundException { } }\nclass C extends P { void doSomething() throws IOException { } }</code></pre>\n<h2>Exceptions in lambdas</h2>\n<p>Java's standard functional interfaces have <strong>no <code>throws</code> clause</strong> — so inside a lambda passed to <code>forEach</code>, <code>map</code> and friends you can only throw <strong>unchecked</strong> exceptions.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">List&lt;Integer&gt; integers = Arrays.asList(3, 9, 7, 0, 10, 20);\nintegers.forEach(i -&gt; {\n    if (i == 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(&quot;Zero not allowed&quot;);\n    System.out.println(Math.PI / i);\n});</code></pre>\n<p>With your <strong>own</strong> functional interface, checked exceptions are possible:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">@FunctionalInterface\npublic interface CheckedFunction&lt;T&gt; {\n    void apply(T t) throws Exception;\n}\n\npublic void processTasks(List&lt;Task&gt; tasks, CheckedFunction&lt;Task&gt; fn) {\n    for (Task task : tasks) {\n        try { fn.apply(task); } catch (Exception e) { /* ... */ }\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<h2>Interview questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Q: How does <code>throw</code> differ from <code>throws</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> <code>throw</code> is a statement — it throws a specific exception object. <code>throws</code> is part of the signature — it tells callers the method may throw that type and, for checked exceptions, forces them to handle it.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Checked or unchecked — how do you choose for a custom exception?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Can the caller realistically react and carry on? If yes — checked. If it is a programming error or an unrecoverable state — unchecked.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Does <code>finally</code> always run?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Almost. It does not run after <code>System.exit()</code>, a fatal JVM failure, a killed process, or a stopped thread. And an exception thrown <strong>inside</strong> <code>finally</code> will mask the one from <code>try</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: What does this code throw?</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">Integer[][] ints = { { 1, 2, 3 }, { null }, { 7, 8, 9 } };\nSystem.out.println(&quot;value = &quot; + ints[1][1].intValue());</code></pre>\n<p><strong>A:</strong> <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>, not <code>NullPointerException</code>. The array <code>ints[1]</code> has <strong>length 1</strong>, so index <code>1</code> is out of bounds before the <code>null</code> is ever dereferenced.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Why is exception handling considered expensive?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> The main cost is <strong>building the stack trace</strong> when the exception object is created — it requires walking the stack. On top of that comes the disruption of normal flow and the loss of JIT optimisation on that path. Which is why an exception is not a flow-control tool.</p>\n<p><strong>Q: Can you throw a checked exception from a method with no <code>throws</code>?</strong> <strong>A:</strong> Yes — a <em>sneaky throw</em>, exploiting type erasure. A curiosity, not a pattern:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">@SuppressWarnings(&quot;unchecked&quot;)\npublic &lt;T extends Throwable&gt; void sneakyThrow(Throwable ex) throws T {\n    throw (T) ex;\n}</code></pre>\n<h2>Best practices</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Catch specifically</strong>, not <code>catch (Exception e)</code>.</li><li><strong>Never an empty <code>catch</code></strong> — a swallowed exception is the most expensive kind of bug.</li><li><strong>Log through SLF4J/Log4j</strong>, not <code>System.out.println</code>. Log the <strong>exception</strong>, not just <code>e.getMessage()</code>, or you lose the stack trace.</li><li><strong>Always <code>try-with-resources</code></strong> instead of a hand-written <code>finally</code> for closing.</li><li><strong>Preserve the cause</strong> when wrapping — <code>new TaskException(msg, ex)</code>, not <code>new TaskException(msg)</code>.</li><li><strong>Custom exceptions</strong> for domain context.</li></ul>\n<h2>Pitfalls</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Parent before child in <code>catch</code></strong> → compile error. Order matters.</li><li><strong><code>return</code> in <code>finally</code></strong> overrides the <code>try</code> value and <strong>swallows the exception</strong>. Never do it.</li><li><strong>Catching <code>Error</code></strong> — technically allowed, almost never sensible.</li><li><strong>Exceptions as flow control</strong> — expensive and unreadable.</li><li><strong><code>e.printStackTrace()</code> in production</strong> — goes to <code>stderr</code> outside the logging system, so it never reaches Kibana.</li></ul>\n<h2>Related</h2>\n<p><span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Type_System_And_Language_Building_Blocks_EN</span> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Memory_Management_And_Garbage_Collection_EN</span> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Java_8_Lambdas_Streams_Functional_Interfaces_EN</span> · <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Exception_Handling_And_IO_PL</span></p>"},"Microservices/Spring Boot - Authentication with JWT_ENG":{"title":"JWT Authentication in Spring Boot Microservices and Alternatives to Keycloak","category":"Microservices","date":"2026-11-06","tags":[],"minutes":4,"lead":"","html":"<h1>Securing Microservices with JWT - Modern Standards</h1>\n<p>According to modern standards (Spring Boot 3.x / Spring Security 6.x), a single microservice <strong>should not generate JWT tokens or manage passwords at all</strong>. Instead, it acts exclusively as an <strong>OAuth2 Resource Server</strong>, while a central Identity Provider (IdP) like Keycloak, Auth0, or Microsoft Entra ID is responsible for authentication and issuing tokens.</p>\n<h2>1. Key Standards and Anti-patterns</h2>\n<ul><li>🛑 <strong>No custom JWT filters (Anti-pattern):</strong> The most common mistake from outdated tutorials is manually writing a <code>JwtAuthenticationFilter</code> class and using libraries like <code>jjwt</code> to verify the signature. Modern Spring Security handles this automatically, much more securely, and efficiently.</li><li>🛑 <strong>Symmetric keys (Anti-pattern):</strong> Using the HS256 algorithm with a hardcoded <code>secret-key</code> is a serious security flaw in a microservices architecture.</li><li>✅ <strong>Asymmetric keys (JWKS):</strong> Modern systems use asymmetric algorithms (RS256). The microservice does not know the private key – it dynamically fetches public keys (JWKS) from the identity server to verify signatures.</li><li>✅ <strong>Strict Claim validation:</strong> A valid signature alone is not enough. The microservice must strictly verify the <code>iss</code> (Issuer - was the token issued by a trusted server?), <code>exp</code> (Expiration), and <code>aud</code> (Audience - was the token generated for this specific service?) claims.</li><li>✅ <strong>Full statelessness:</strong> The microservice must completely disable HTTP sessions (<code>STATELESS</code>), relying entirely on the <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> header for each request.</li></ul>\n<h2>2. Implementation in Spring Boot</h2>\n<p>You only need one additional starter to enable native Resource Server support.</p>\n<h3>Dependencies (Maven)</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-xml\">&lt;dependency&gt;\n    &lt;groupId&gt;org.springframework.boot&lt;/groupId&gt;\n    &lt;artifactId&gt;spring-boot-starter-security&lt;/artifactId&gt;\n&lt;/dependency&gt;\n&lt;dependency&gt;\n    &lt;groupId&gt;org.springframework.boot&lt;/groupId&gt;\n    &lt;artifactId&gt;spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server&lt;/artifactId&gt;\n&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>\n<h3>Configuration (<code>application.yml</code>)</h3>\n<p>Instead of providing secret keys, you only provide the URL of your identity server. Spring Boot will automatically fetch the configuration (OIDC Discovery) and public keys on startup.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">spring:\n  security:\n    oauth2:\n      resourceserver:\n        jwt:\n          issuer-uri: https://your-auth-server.com/realms/myrealm # E.g., your IdP URL\n          audiences: my-microservice-audience # Optional, but highly recommended</code></pre>\n<h3>Security Configuration (Java)</h3>\n<p>The code configuring the security filter is fully based on Spring's built-in DSL.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-java\">import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;\nimport org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;\nimport org.springframework.security.config.Customizer;\nimport org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;\nimport org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.configuration.EnableWebSecurity;\nimport org.springframework.security.config.http.SessionCreationPolicy;\nimport org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;\n\n@Configuration\n@EnableWebSecurity\npublic class SecurityConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n        http\n            // 1. Secure endpoints\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -&gt; auth\n                .requestMatchers(&quot;/api/public/**&quot;).permitAll()\n                .requestMatchers(&quot;/api/admin/**&quot;).hasRole(&quot;ADMIN&quot;)\n                .anyRequest().authenticated()\n            )\n            // 2. Enable OAuth2 Resource Server and JWT support\n            .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -&gt; oauth2\n                .jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()) // Uses issuer-uri from application.yml by default\n            )\n            // 3. Enforce stateless architecture\n            .sessionManagement(session -&gt; session\n                .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS)\n            )\n            // 4. Disable CSRF (not needed with stateless token-based authentication)\n            .csrf(csrf -&gt; csrf.disable());\n\n        return http.build();\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><em>Tip: By default, Spring Security looks for permissions in the <code>scope</code> or <code>scp</code> claim. If your identity provider hides roles deeper within the token's JSON structure, you will need to write a class that implements <code>JwtAuthenticationConverter</code>.</em></p>\n<h2>3. Lightweight and Free Alternatives to Keycloak</h2>\n<p>Keycloak is a powerful tool, but it can be too heavy, complex to configure, and resource-intensive for simple, custom projects. For web and mobile applications, modern CIAM (Customer Identity and Access Management) solutions are a great fit and can be easily self-hosted using Docker.</p>\n<h3>Option 1: Logto (Best for B2C and Frontend Developers)</h3>\n<p>Designed specifically for developers building consumer-facing apps.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Appearance:</strong> Features highly modern and minimalist out-of-the-box login screens. You don't have to code your own authentication UI.</li><li><strong>Integration:</strong> Provides dedicated, easy-to-use SDKs for React, Vue, Flutter, iOS, and Android.</li><li><strong>Features:</strong> Focuses on password login, Social Login (Google, Apple), registration flows, and password recovery.</li></ul>\n<h3>Option 2: ZITADEL (Performance and Modernity)</h3>\n<p>A rapidly growing alternative to Keycloak.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Performance:</strong> Written in Go, meaning it consumes a fraction of the RAM/CPU compared to Java-based solutions.</li><li><strong>Innovation (Passkeys):</strong> Offers native, excellent support for biometric login (WebAuthn / FaceID / TouchID) right out of the box.</li><li><strong>Scalability:</strong> Includes advanced organization management (making it a great choice for future B2B systems).</li></ul>\n<h3>Option 3: Pocket ID (Absolute Minimalism)</h3>\n<p>The perfect solution for hobby projects where you want to ditch passwords entirely.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Operation:</strong> An ultra-lightweight OIDC provider (Go + SQLite) designed <strong>exclusively</strong> for login via biometrics or hardware keys (Passkeys).</li><li><strong>Simplicity:</strong> Zero complex roles or transactional emails. The user scans their fingerprint, and the app receives a valid JWT token ready for your Spring Boot backend.</li></ul>"},"System design/Common problems - ENG":{"title":"Common problems - ENG","category":"System design","date":"","tags":[],"minutes":3,"lead":"","html":"<h1>System Design – Interview Compendium</h1>\n<ul><li><strong>Slow reads</strong> ➔ <code>Add a cache</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Slow read operations are optimized by introducing a caching layer.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> It is worth discussing invalidation strategies (Cache-Aside, Write-Through) and managing eventual consistency using in-memory data stores (e.g., Redis, Memcached).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Write spikes</strong> ➔ <code>Buffer with a queue</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Sudden surges in write requests are mitigated by buffering the traffic through a message queue.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Utilizing a message broker (e.g., <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Apache Kafka</span>) protects the database from being overwhelmed, but requires adopting an asynchronous processing model.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Too much traffic</strong> ➔ <code>Add a load balancer</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Excessive incoming traffic is managed by implementing a Load Balancer to distribute requests across multiple instances.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Maintaining stateless server architecture is crucial, along with knowledge of traffic distribution algorithms (L4 vs L7, e.g., Ingress controllers in <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Kubernetes</span>).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Read-heavy DB</strong> ➔ <code>Add read replicas</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Scaling a database dominated by read queries is achieved by adding read replicas.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Emphasize the <em>replication lag</em> problem (e.g., in <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">PostgreSQL</span>) and the architectural pattern of segregating <code>SELECT</code> queries from state-mutating commands (CQRS).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Write-heavy DB</strong> ➔ <code>Shard the data</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> When the database bottleneck is caused by massive write volumes, the optimal solution is data partitioning (sharding).</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Point out the operational complexity of selecting an appropriate <em>Shard Key</em> and the inability to easily execute cross-node <code>JOIN</code> operations.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Slow queries</strong> ➔ <code>Add indexes</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Poorly performing database queries are optimized by creating appropriate indexes.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> A B-Tree index significantly accelerates data retrieval but introduces a performance penalty for write operations (<code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>) and consumes additional storage space.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Slow file loads</strong> ➔ <code>Object storage + CDN</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Sluggish static asset loading is resolved by placing files in object storage combined with a Content Delivery Network (CDN).</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Highlight the architectural advantage of caching and serving static content at edge nodes closest to the end user, utilizing cloud solutions (e.g., <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">GCP</span> Cloud Storage).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Temporary failures</strong> ➔ <code>Retry with backoff</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> The system should handle transient network errors by applying a delayed retry strategy.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Always mention adding a randomization factor (<em>Jitter</em>) alongside an <em>Exponential Backoff</em> algorithm to protect recovering downstream services from synchronized retry floods (<em>thundering herd problem</em>).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Too many requests</strong> ➔ <code>Rate limiting</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> The system protects its resources from excessive incoming requests by enforcing throughput limits.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> This pattern shields API Gateways from abuse and DDoS attacks; familiarity with algorithms like <em>Token Bucket</em> or <em>Leaky Bucket</em> is highly valued.</li></ul></li><li><strong>One service failing</strong> ➔ <code>Circuit breaker</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> A single component's failure is isolated to prevent cascading system outages using the circuit breaker pattern.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Demonstrate an understanding of the <em>fail-fast</em> philosophy that prevents thread pool exhaustion (e.g., in <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Spring Boot</span> microservices) and discuss the states: closed, open, and half-open (e.g., using Resilience4j).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Duplicate requests</strong> ➔ <code>Idempotency keys</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> To prevent unintended double processing of operations, API requests are secured with idempotency keys.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> This is a fundamental requirement in event-driven systems, ensuring that duplicate messages do not compromise state consistency (especially critical in financial transactions).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Can't see what broke</strong> ➔ <code>Logs, metrics, tracing</code><ul><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Difficulties in diagnosing root causes are resolved by implementing a comprehensive observability stack: logs, metrics, and distributed tracing.</li><li><strong>Interview aspects:</strong> Centralized observability involves aggregating full-text logs (e.g., <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Elasticsearch</span>), visualizing telemetry data on dashboards (e.g., <span class=\"missing-link\" title=\"Brak notatki w datasource\">Grafana</span>), and propagating <code>Trace ID</code> headers across distributed microservices.</li></ul></li></ul>"},"about":{"title":"About me","category":"","date":"","tags":[],"minutes":1,"lead":"[KRÓTKI OPIS — kim jesteś i o czym jest ten blog]","html":"<h2>Czym jest ten blog</h2>\n<p>MBS. Knowledge Hub Blog to publiczna część mojego notatnika technicznego. Struktura kategorii po lewej stronie odwzorowuje katalogi w lokalnym Knowledge Hubie.</p>\n<h2>Kontakt</h2>\n<p>[ADRES E-MAIL / LINKEDIN / GITHUB]</p>"}}};
